llvm-3.7.1 local rule: JumpThreading does not duplicate a block that holds
an atomic read-modify-write or a compare-exchange
(no upstream state)

This patch CHANGES JUMPTHREADING ONLY (getJumpThreadDuplicationCost, the
cost both of its duplications ask: ThreadEdge, JumpThreading.cpp:1383, and
DuplicateCondBranchOnPHIIntoPred, :1526). One change, nothing else:
  JumpThreading.cpp, getJumpThreadDuplicationCost: a block holding an
    atomicrmw or a cmpxchg costs ~0U, as a block holding a call that cannot
    be duplicated does (:271-274), so it is never duplicated.
Unchanged: every other cost and the threshold (6); SimplifyCFG's
FoldCondBranchOnPHI (SimplifyCFG.cpp:1680), which still copies such a block
per predecessor when the branch is on an i1 phi of the block with one use
and no value of the block is used outside it; fences and atomic loads and
stores. The pass's own rule: the unit pipeline's two runs and the link's
two take it. The library line (3.3) is another compiler. src/sce/ and
src/game/sndx/ are built by this compiler and take the rule.

Question (rounds 372B, 426C, 429C, 430C, 566D): the base leaves the reader
count's release (`lock dec` of the count FUN_01331c10 took) as one block
before a branch on the pointer a search found, where 3.7.1 copies it into
each predecessor that brings a constant null (the release block {phi,
atomicrmw sub, icmp, br} costs 2 of 6). Base census (scratch d566/sites.txt,
step1_table.txt): 1,579 release sites in 673 functions; 832 in 441
functions are followed by a branch on the found pointer with one or more
predecessors bringing a constant and are not copied. 8 functions copy their
release (0x4ba970, 0x7b2d50, 0x9f6bc0, 0x9f8a90, 0xb88680, 0xc1efc0,
0xc8b960, 0xcefda0), each with one signature: the copy left tests the
pointer and never uses it, an i1 phi with one use that SimplifyCFG's
FoldCondBranchOnPHI copies one predecessor at a time (0x9f6bc0's second
search: three releases, 0x9f6c73, 0x9f6c7e, 0x9f6c8b, where ThreadEdge
would factor the predecessors of one value into one copy, :1177-1198).

Evidence (scratch d566/). JumpThreading.cpp with switches linked into a
copy of the installed opt (its own objects; control: 6,580 of 6,580 unit
bitcode files and the link stage's module byte-identical to the tree's, 0
functions differ). The whole game module with this rule at both stages:
  22 symbols change, 0 matched rows change (0 rows lost); 1 draft equal
    (FUN_0046a860), 11 closer (FUN_00ad11a0 and its three copies 0.62 to
    0.89, FUN_0046f770 and FUN_0046f980 0.20 to 0.39, FUN_00857df0 and
    FUN_00857f80 0.26 to 0.46, FUN_005065f0 0.07 to 0.42, FUN_008b1d60 and
    FUN_008b2280 0.06 to 0.12), 1 lower by score only (FUN_00765280: now the
    base's structure, one release, the test after it, the loop rotated; its
    registers shift by the count's address kept, a separate residual).
  A second kind of site: FUN_0133fb30's unlock cmpxchg before a `test dl,
    dl` (block cost 1) is copied into both arms by 3.7.1 and is one block
    in the base (0x133fc4c); the rule gives the base's one.
  At stage 1 only, the link's JumpThreading copies all but 3 again: the
    rule is the pass's, at both stages.
  The 8 copying functions are unchanged (SimplifyCFG copies them).
  The first search of 0x9f6bc0, 0x9f8a90 and 0xb88680 (and 0xa2fca0's last)
    keeps its bool (`test rdi, rdi; sete al` before one release, `test al,
    al` after it): the same release block. A probe of 0x9f6bc0 (scratch
    d567/probe/) with the bool held in a variable the return reads (`bool r
    = !node->findComponent(A); if (r) ...; return r;`) gives the base's
    first search instruction for instruction under this rule but for the
    release's address operand, and copies it under 3.7.1 (two releases: the
    phi has two uses, so only JumpThreading can copy it).

Source forms and rules the bytes exclude: seventeen source forms of the
release and its branch (rounds 372B and 426C to 430C, among them the found
pointer carried in the loop's condition, the release written on each
return, a scoped guard, the release defined once out of line, a release
ordering for the fall) and the release written as asm (430C, four operand
forms) all give the copy; a cost charged for each atomic
(+5: FUN_0133fb30's block costs 6 and is still copied); a compare of a phi
evaluated per predecessor only when the phi has one use (229 rows lost);
the rule at stage 1 only (the link copies again). Whether a fence or an
atomic load or store is refused too, or only an atomic whose result is
unused, the module cannot tell (all three give the same functions); the
rule takes the read-modify-write and the compare-exchange, the two the
sites show.

NO upstream state. No release of JumpThreading from 3.3 to 4.0.0 refuses an
atomic: only a call that cannot be duplicated (and, from 4.0.0, a
convergent one). Sony authorship of this is not shown.

Refuted by: one base site where a block holding an atomicrmw or a cmpxchg
is duplicated by a jump thread (a branch on a pointer, an integer compare or
a phi with other uses, the predecessors of one value factored into one
copy), not by SimplifyCFG's fold of an i1 phi with one use.

--- a/lib/Transforms/Scalar/JumpThreading.cpp
+++ b/lib/Transforms/Scalar/JumpThreading.cpp
@@ -249,6 +249,11 @@
   unsigned Size = 0;
   for (; !isa<TerminatorInst>(I); ++I) {
 
+    // Local rule (jumpthread-01): a block that holds an atomic
+    // read-modify-write is never duplicated, as a call that cannot be.
+    if (isa<AtomicRMWInst>(I) || isa<AtomicCmpXchgInst>(I))
+      return ~0U;
+
     // Stop scanning the block if we've reached the threshold.
     if (Size > Threshold)
       return Size;
